 "title": "Handling Job Offers and Retention Conversations",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "An employee informs their manager about an outside offer with a 30% salary jump and a senior title. Instead of demanding a match immediately, the employee expresses a preference to stay and asks the manager to speak with the founder by Friday.",
 "key_points": [
  "The employee received an outside offer with a 30% jump in current CTC and a senior engineer title.",
  "The outside company gave the employee until Friday to respond.",
  "The employee wants to stay because they like the company and know the team well.",
  "The employee asks the manager to speak with the founder to discuss the situation.",
  "The manager asks for 24 hours to look into the matter."
 ],
 "content": "An employee tells their manager, \"Boss, I received an offer from another company.\" The manager asks if they are serious about it. The employee confirms they have the offer letter and need to respond by Friday, but wanted to speak to the manager first. When the manager asks about the offer, the employee says, \"It is a 30% jump on my current CTC. And a senior engineer title.\" The manager replies that it is a big number and doubts they can match it. The employee clarifies, \"I'm not asking you to match it today, but if you can maybe chat on this with the founder and let me know by Friday.\" The employee explains they want to stay because they like it there and know the team well. The manager responds, \"Okay, how about you give me 24 hours?\" The employee agrees and thanks the manager.",
 "status": "done",
